# How can Achilles ever catch the tortoise? Zeno's paradox
tags: ["infinite series","limits","achilles and the tortoise","history of mathematics"]
teaches: ["concepts/infinite-series","concepts/limits","history/zeno-to-cauchy"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "If every gap takes time to close, how can infinitely many gaps take finite time? Why did it take until the 1800s to say what an infinite sum means?"
next: {"title":"Can a full hotel take one more guest? Hilbert's Hotel and Cantor's diagonal","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/02-can-a-full-hotel-take-one-more-guest.cast"}

## How can Achilles ever catch the tortoise? Zeno's paradox
domain: {"x":[-1,9],"y":[0,13]}
vars: {"w":1.5}
sources:
    - id: aristotle_phys
      title: Physics, Book VI ch. 9 (239b14) and Book VIII ch. 8
      authors: Aristotle
      year: -350
      finding: Reports Zeno's Achilles argument; answers with potential versus actual infinity.
    - id: archimedes_qp
      title: Quadrature of the Parabola
      authors: Archimedes
      finding: Parabolic segment = 4/3 of a triangle, via 1 + 1/4 + 1/16 + … by exhaustion.
    - id: cauchy1821
      title: Cours d'analyse de l'École royale polytechnique
      authors: Augustin-Louis Cauchy
      year: 1821
      finding: A series' sum is the limit of its partial sums; 1 − 1 + 1 − … has none.
    - id: weierstrass
      title: Karl Weierstrass (Wikipedia)
      authors: Wikipedia
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Weierstrass
      finding: Introduced the epsilon-delta definition of limit in 1861.
    - id: sep_zeno
      title: Zeno's Paradoxes
      authors: Nick Huggett (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
      url: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paradox-zeno/
      finding: Zeno (b. c. 490 BC) defended Parmenides; Cauchy's sums are the received resolution; debate continues.
    - id: grandi
      title: History of Grandi's series (Wikipedia)
      authors: Wikipedia
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Grandi%27s_series
      finding: Grandi (1703) argued 1 − 1 + 1 − … equals 1/2.

