    flip ach tort axis vertical duration 0

    card title "Can Achilles catch the tortoise?"

A: (@draw lane_a ach@) Can the fastest runner in Greek legend ever catch a  (@draw lane_t tort@)tortoise that starts ahead of him?

A: Of course he can. Yet about two and a half tho (@draw zeno@)usand years ago, Zeno of Elea argued that he never will.

A: Zeno was defending his teacher Parmenides, who held that motion is an illusion, and this race was his weapon.
    highlight zeno

    erase zeno

A: Let's give it numbers. Achilles runs t (@draw sp_a@)en metres a second. The tortoise, an unusually (@draw sp_t@) fit one, runs one metre a second, and st (@draw gap1@)arts a hundred metres ahead.

    erase sp_a sp_t

A: So here's Zeno (@move tort by [220,0] duration 2.5@)'s argument. Firs (@move ach by [430,0] ghost true duration 2@)t, Achilles must reach the spot where the tortoise started.

A: That takes him ten seconds. But in those ten se (@highlight tort@)conds the (@draw gap2@) tortoise has crawled on, ten metres.

A: So h (@move tort by [110,0] duration 1.4@)e runs those t (@move ach by [220,0] duration 1.2@)en metres, in one second. By then t (@draw gap3@)he tortoise has gone one more metre.

A: (@move tort by [55,0] duration 0.7@) Then a ten (@move ach by [110,0] duration 0.7@)th of a me (@draw gap4@)tre, then a hu (@move tort by [30,0] duration 0.5@)ndredth. E (@move ach by [55,0] duration 0.5@)ach time h (@draw gap5 not_scale@)e reaches where it was, it has already left.

A: There's always another gap, so there are infinitely many of them.
    highlight gaps

A: (@draw c1@) And each gap takes some time.  (@draw ca1 c2@)Infinitely many pieces of time, says Zeno,  (@draw ca2 c3@)add up to forever, so Achilles never catches up.

A: Each step sounds  (@highlight c2 duration 1.6@)innocent, and the conclusion is (@highlight c3 duration 2.5@) plainly false, so one step must be wrong. Finding which one is what this course is about.
    highlight c1 duration 1.6

A: Our route: add up the time, see why that sum took mathematicians until the 1800s to define, then find the broken step.
    flow ca1 ca2 duration 4

    clear

    card title "Add up the time"

A: (@draw tline@) Start with the time. The first gap to (@draw s1 s1_t@)ok Achilles ten seconds.

A: (@draw f1@) The second took (@draw f2@) one second, the thi (@draw f3@)rd a tenth of a second, and so  (@draw f4@)on, each a tenth of the one before.

A: Infinitely many pieces of time. Do they really add up to forever?
    highlight formula

    ask "Click where you think the total time ends up. Far right if you think it never ends." on tline store g default "14" after keep gap false voice a
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it's {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it's {g.true}. Each new piece adds only a tenth of the one before.

A: Watch the running totals (@draw tot1@): ten seconds, then (@draw s2 s2_t tot2@) eleven, then eleve (@draw s3 s3_t tot3@)n point one, eleven  (@draw tot4@)point one one.

A: They keep gr (@draw tot5@)owing, but by less each time,  (@erase formula@)and they never pass ele (@draw wall@)ven and one ninth seconds: eleven point one recurring.

A: Check it the plain wa (@draw calc1@)y: Achilles gains nine metres every second on a hundred-metre  (@draw calc2@)lead, and a hundred divided by nine is eleven point one.

A: Same numbe (@highlight tline_answer@)r. After eleven point one seconds he has run a hun (@draw level@)dred and eleven metres, and draws level.

    erase calc

A: So the infinitely many steps are real, but they shrink so fast that together they fill a finite time.
    highlight s1 s2 s3 wall

A: But what does it even mean to add up infinitely many numbers?
    highlight tot5

    clear

    card title "Why it took so long"

A: (@draw tl tl_br tl_br2 tl2@) You can never finish the adding, and for a (@draw zn@)bout two thousand years, nobody could say.

A: (@draw ar@) Aristotle, a century after Zeno, said the (@draw ar_c@) infinity is only potential: you can div (@highlight ar_c@)ide a line for ever in thought, but never all at once.

A: (@draw am@) Archimedes, a century later, showed (@draw am_c@) that a quarter plus a sixteenth plus a sixty-fourth, and so on, comes to a t (@highlight am_c@)hird, but he proved it by squeezing, never by adding forever.

A: In the 1600s (@draw nl@) Newton and Leibniz built calculus on infinite  (@flow tl2 duration 2.5@)sums, and used them freely, mostly with the right answers.

A: Mostly. In 1703 the  (@draw gr@)Italian monk Guido Grandi looked a (@draw gr_c@)t one minus one plus one minus one, for ever.

A: Pair t (@draw grandi_s_box grandi_s_line_1@)he terms one way and you get zero. Shift  (@draw grandi_s_line_2@)the brackets by one and you get one. Grandi him (@highlight gr_c@)self argued for a half.

A: One sum, three answers. Adding for ever meant nothing until someone said what it means.
    highlight grandi_s

    erase grandi_s

A: That was (@draw cauchy@) Augustin-Louis Cauchy, in his 1821 t (@draw cy@)extbook for students in Paris.

    clear

    card title "What a sum is"

A: (@draw ax@) His idea: don't try to add for ever. Write down the running totals, and ask where they settle.

A: Achilles' (@draw a1 a2 a3@)s totals climb toward ele (@draw a4 a5 a6 a7 a8 ach_l@)ven  (@draw lim lim_t@)point one recurring, and get ever closer to it.

A: Draw a ban (@draw bu bl band@)d around that line, (@animate w 0.3 duration 2.2@) as thin as you like. From some step on, every total stays inside it.

A: That's a lim (@draw sum_l@)it, and the sum is the limit. Nothing has to be fin (@highlight lim lim_t@)ished: the sum is the number the totals close in on.

A: Cauchy (@highlight sum_l@) still spoke of quantities 'infinitely small'; by 1861 (@draw weier@) Karl Weierstrass had turned it int (@highlight band a3 a4 a5 a6 a7 a8@)o this strict band test.

A: Now Grandi's tot (@draw g1 g2 g3 g4@) (@draw g5 g6 g7 g8 gr_l@)als: one, zero, one, zero, for ever.

    ask "Which running totals have a sum? Tap them." choose ["ach_pts","gr_pts"] answer ach_pts store which voice a
        right: Achilles's: they settle on 11.11…. Grandi's jump between 0 and 1 for ever.
        wrong: Look for the dots that end up inside one thin band.

A: They never settle, so under Cauchy's rule, Gra (@draw nosum_l@)ndi's series has no sum at all.

    erase which_ring

    clear

    card title "Can Achilles catch the tortoise?"

A: (@draw e1 ea1 e2@) Most mathematicians say (@draw ea2 e3@) that settles Zeno. Some philosophers still ask how anyone comp (@highlight e1@)letes infinitely many tasks; the usual reply is that Achi (@highlight e2 e3@)lles doesn't do them one by one, he simply runs.

A: So the broken step was the qui (@highlight ea2@)et one: that infinitely many pieces of time m (@draw ea2_x@)ust add up to forever. Pieces that sh (@draw e_fix@)rink fast enough have  (@draw e3_x@)a finite total.

A: Fast enough matter (@draw e_harm@)s: a half plus a third plus a quarter, and so on, shrinks too, yet (@draw e_harm2@) it creeps past any number you name.

A: And saying exactly what that total is gave mathematics the limit, the idea underneath all of calculus.
    highlight e_fix

    quiz "Which of these sums has a finite total?" voice a
        right: 0.9 + 0.09 + … settles on 1. The halves-thirds-quarters sum creeps past any number you name, and Grandi's never settles.
        wrong: Shrinking pieces aren't always enough. Picture the running totals: do they settle on one number?
        * 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/4 + …
        + 0.9 + 0.09 + 0.009 + …
        * 1 − 1 + 1 − 1 + …

    erase e_harm e_harm2

    flip f_ach f_tort axis vertical duration 0

A: (@draw ftrk f_ach f_tort@) So yes, Achilles ca (@move f_ach by [620,0] duration 3@) (@move f_tort by [60,0] duration 3@)tches the tortoise, after eleven (@draw f_lab@) point one seconds. Explaining how took mathematics about two thousand years.

