      mp3: 
      ms: 8720
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:68g8fh'
    '|a||Each step sounds innocent, and the conclusion is plainly false, so one step must be wrong. Finding which one is what this course is about.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9720
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1kvjfsx'
    '|a||Our route: add up the time, see why that sum took mathematicians until the 1800s to define, then find the broken step.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:ug3q7x'
    '|a||Start with the time. The first gap took Achilles ten seconds.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4240
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1kzmb0e'
    '|a||The second took one second, the third a tenth of a second, and so on, each a tenth of the one before.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:12ybdox'
    '|a||Infinitely many pieces of time. Do they really add up to forever?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1dylwa2'
    '|a||Click where you think the total time ends up. Far right if you think it never ends.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1q7igb2'
    '|a||Watch the running totals: ten seconds, then eleven, then eleven point one, eleven point one one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||They keep growing, but by less each time, and they never pass eleven and one ninth seconds: eleven point one recurring.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:598q95'
    '|a||Check it the plain way: Achilles gains nine metres every second on a hundred-metre lead, and a hundred divided by nine is eleven point one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:zepad5'
    '|a||Same number. After eleven point one seconds he has run a hundred and eleven metres, and draws level.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:ovq0m8'
    '|a||So the infinitely many steps are real, but they shrink so fast that together they fill a finite time.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:12k5ltc'
    '|a||But what does it even mean to add up infinitely many numbers?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3520
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1l7pxqt'
    '|a||You can never finish the adding, and for about two thousand years, nobody could say.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:2rkxa2'
    '|a||Aristotle, a century after Zeno, said the infinity is only potential: you can divide a line for ever in thought, but never all at once.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8720
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1ipka24'
    '|a||Archimedes, a century later, showed that a quarter plus a sixteenth plus a sixty-fourth, and so on, comes to a third, but he proved it by squeezing, never by adding forever.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12160
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:jgu2vo'
    '|a||In the 1600s Newton and Leibniz built calculus on infinite sums, and used them freely, mostly with the right answers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7240
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:utc15w'
    '|a||Mostly. In 1703 the Italian monk Guido Grandi looked at one minus one plus one minus one, for ever.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:fso5gp'
    '|a||Pair the terms one way and you get zero. Shift the brackets by one and you get one. Grandi himself argued for a half.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1onyaaz'
    '|a||One sum, three answers. Adding for ever meant nothing until someone said what it means.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:d3p7q6'
    '|a||That was Augustin-Louis Cauchy, in his 1821 textbook for students in Paris.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5160
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:474egc'
    '|a||His idea: don''t try to add for ever. Write down the running totals, and ask where they settle.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6680
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:17e8664'
    '|a||Achilles''s totals climb toward eleven point one recurring, and get ever closer to it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:bh1myd'
    '|a||Draw a band around that line, as thin as you like. From some step on, every total stays inside it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:fryqin'
    '|a||That''s a limit, and the sum is the limit. Nothing has to be finished: the sum is the number the totals close in on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1e5srw'
    '|a||Cauchy still spoke of quantities ''infinitely small''; by 1861 Karl Weierstrass had turned it into this strict band test.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:irahij'
    '|a||Now Grandi''s totals: one, zero, one, zero, for ever.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4320
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:kmx127'
    '|a||Which running totals have a sum? Tap them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:bgfpff'
    '|a||Achilles''s: they settle on 11.11…. Grandi''s jump between 0 and 1 for ever.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6520
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||They never settle, so under Cauchy''s rule, Grandi''s series has no sum at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5480
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Most mathematicians say that settles Zeno. Some philosophers still ask how anyone completes infinitely many tasks; the usual reply is that Achilles doesn''t do them one by one, he simply runs.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:xw23fo'
    '|a||So the broken step was the quiet one: that infinitely many pieces of time must add up to forever. Pieces that shrink fast enough have a finite total.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9960
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1gx2hy9'
    '|a||Fast enough matters: a half plus a third plus a quarter, and so on, shrinks too, yet it creeps past any number you name.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:co8cak'
    '|a||And saying exactly what that total is gave mathematics the limit, the idea underneath all of calculus.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7520
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1sk0l8x'
    '|a||Which of these sums has a finite total?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2600
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:18q4c8o'
    '|a||0.9 + 0.09 + … settles on 1. The halves-thirds-quarters sum creeps past any number you name, and Grandi''s never settles.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11120
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||So yes, Achilles catches the tortoise, after eleven point one seconds. Explaining how took mathematics about two thousand years.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9120
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Next: Can a full hotel take one more guest? Hilbert''s Hotel and Cantor''s diagonal':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6240
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Look for the dots that end up inside one thin band.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3440
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
